\description{
Checks an SBOM file against the schema of its format, and checks the
content of a file or of an \verb{<sbom>} object against integrity rules and the
NTIA 2021 minimum elements. A file can come from sbom or from any other
tool, in any of the eight formats that \code{\link[=read_sbom]{read_sbom()}} reads.
}
\details{
\code{validate_sbom()} runs two layers of checks on a file.

The \strong{schema layer} checks the file against the schema of its format and
version. sbom bundles the schemas, so validation works offline:
\itemize{
\item CycloneDX JSON, SPDX JSON and YAML, and SPDX 3 JSON-LD are checked with
jsonvalidate, which must be installed.
\item CycloneDX XML and SWID tags are checked against their XSDs with xml2.
\item SPDX has no official schema for XML or tag-value, so those files are
read into the SPDX JSON model and checked against the SPDX JSON schema.
At best they are \code{partial}.
\item SPDX 3 is also \code{partial} at best, since SPDX's SHACL rules are not
checked.
}

A version with no bundled schema, such as CycloneDX 1.3, is not checked,
and a note says so. Each finding of this layer has the check \code{schema}. It
is an error when the file fails its schema, and otherwise a note that says
what could not be checked.

The \strong{content layer} reads the file as \code{\link[=read_sbom]{read_sbom()}} does, and checks the
values the document itself holds, not the \verb{<sbom>} that \code{read_sbom()} makes.
So nothing \code{read_sbom()} leaves out can hide a problem. The integrity checks
always run: unique ids, relationships that name elements of the document,
licence expressions against the bundled SPDX License List, purls, and the
names and versions of R packages. The profile adds its own checks. The
layer fails when any of its findings is an error; warnings and notes do not
fail it.

When the file cannot be read, as when it is not an SBOM, the result is
\code{fail} with one finding, of check \code{read}, and nothing else runs.

An \verb{<sbom>} object gets the content checks only, on its components,
relationships and metadata, since it has no file to check against a schema.
With the default \code{checks} the schema layer is left out, and asking for it is
an error. To check both, write the object with \code{\link[=write_sbom]{write_sbom()}} and validate
the file. An \verb{<sbom>} from \code{\link[=read_sbom]{read_sbom()}} holds values that \code{read_sbom()}
supplies, such as a timestamp when the document has none, a placeholder
root and sbom as the tool, and lacks those it cannot keep, such as CPE
identifiers, which may be a component's only identifier. Either changes
the result, so validate the file itself to check the document as it was
written.
}
\section{Statuses}{

Each layer that runs gives a status, and the result's status joins them:
\itemize{
\item \code{"fail"}: a layer failed. The file could not be read, it fails its
schema, or its content has an error.
\item \code{"skipped"}: no layer failed, and every layer that ran was skipped. A
schema layer is skipped when jsonvalidate is not installed, when sbom
bundles no schema for the version, when the document is too large, when
the JSON schema check could not run, or when an XML document uses
entities declared in its DTD, which sbom does not expand.
\item \code{"partial"}: no layer failed, but one was skipped or could check only
part of the rules (SPDX XML, SPDX tag-value and SPDX 3).
\item \code{"pass"}: every layer that ran passed.
}
}

\section{The NTIA minimum elements}{

The \code{"ntia-2021"} profile checks the minimum elements of the NTIA's 2021
report, "The Minimum Elements For a Software Bill of Materials". A value is
present when it is not empty and not \code{NOASSERTION}. A value asserted with an
override counts like any other. The component checks apply to components,
not to files and snippets (SPDX files, and CycloneDX components of type
\code{file}). The relationships are those of the primary component, the one the
document describes. Each element is read from these fields:\tabular{lllll}{
   Element \tab CycloneDX \tab SPDX 2 \tab SPDX 3 \tab SWID \cr
   Supplier \tab \code{supplier}, \code{manufacturer}, \code{publisher} or an author \tab \code{supplier} or \code{originator} \tab \code{suppliedBy} or \code{originatedBy} \tab a \code{distributor}, \code{softwareCreator} or \code{maintainer} entity \cr
   Name \tab \code{name} \tab \code{name} \tab \code{name} \tab \code{name} \cr
   Version \tab \code{version} \tab \code{versionInfo} \tab \code{software_packageVersion} \tab \code{version} \cr
   Identifier \tab \code{purl}, \code{cpe} or \code{swid} \tab a purl, CPE or SWID reference \tab a purl, CPE or SWID identifier \tab \code{tagId} \cr
   Relationships \tab its \code{dependencies} entry, or nested components \tab any but \code{DESCRIBES} \tab any but \code{describes} \tab \code{requires} and \code{component} links \cr
   Author \tab \code{metadata.authors} or \code{metadata.manufacturer} \tab a \verb{Person:} or \verb{Organization:} creator \tab a person or organization in \code{createdBy} \tab the \code{tagCreator} entity \cr
   Timestamp \tab \code{metadata.timestamp} \tab \code{creationInfo.created} \tab \code{created} \tab none \cr
}


A document that names only tools as its authors (CycloneDX tools, SPDX 2
\verb{Tool:} creators, SPDX 3 software agents) gets a warning rather than an
error, since the NTIA asks for the person or organization that made the
SBOM data. For an \verb{<sbom>} object, the elements are each component's
\code{supplier_name} or \code{maintainer}, \code{name}, \code{version}, \code{purl} and
relationships, and the metadata's \code{author} or \code{organization} and
\code{timestamp}; sbom itself is a tool author.

A SWID tag describes only itself, so only the tag gets the component
checks, and a note counts the tags it links to. It has no field for the
time it was made, so \strong{a SWID tag cannot meet the NTIA minimum elements}:
under \code{"ntia-2021"} it always fails with an \code{ntia-timestamp} error.
}

\section{Checks}{

Each finding has one of these checks:
\itemize{
\item \code{read} (error): the file is not an SBOM in a format sbom reads.
\item \code{schema}: an error where the file fails its schema; otherwise a note.
\item \code{duplicate-id} (error): elements share an id.
\item \code{dangling-relationship} (error): a relationship names an element the
document does not define. An element of another document is not
checked: one named through an SPDX \verb{DocumentRef-} or an SPDX 3 import,
or a CycloneDX BOM-Link (\code{urn:cdx:...}), as in a VEX document.
\item \code{license-invalid} (error): a licence expression is not a valid SPDX
expression.
\item \code{license-unknown} (error): it uses an id that is not on the SPDX License
List.
\item \code{license-deprecated} (warning): it uses a deprecated SPDX id.
\item \code{license-ref-undefined} (error, SPDX 2 only): it uses a \verb{LicenseRef-}
that the document does not define in \code{hasExtractedLicensingInfos}.
\item \code{purl-invalid} (error): a purl does not parse.
\item \code{r-package-name} (error): a component whose purl says it is a CRAN
package has a name that is not a valid R package name.
\item \code{r-version} (warning): such a component has a version that is not an R
package version.
\item \code{r-purl-name} (warning): its purl names another package.
\item \code{ntia-author}, \code{ntia-timestamp}, \code{ntia-supplier}, \code{ntia-name},
\code{ntia-version}, \code{ntia-identifier} and \code{ntia-relationships} (errors): an
NTIA minimum element is missing. \code{ntia-author} is a warning when the
document names only tools.
\item \code{swid-links} (note): the tags a SWID tag links to were not checked.
}
}

\section{Limits}{

\itemize{
\item The JSON schema check runs ajv inside V8, and its time grows
quadratically with the length of a CycloneDX component array: about 45
seconds at 20,000 components. The content layer takes seconds; use
\code{checks = "content"} to leave the schema out for a large SBOM.
\item V8 cannot hold a very long string, and trying aborts R. A document whose
JSON, escaped for V8, is longer than 32 MB is not checked against its
schema: the schema layer is skipped, with a note, and the content checks
still run.
\item SPDX YAML nested more than 200 levels deep, or holding more than one YAML
document, is refused before it is parsed, from a scan of its text that
follows libyaml's rules. A crafted SPDX YAML file can still take tens of
seconds to refuse.
\item The JSON schema check calls the validator that jsonvalidate keeps inside
V8, which is not part of jsonvalidate's documented interface. If a
version of jsonvalidate changes it, the schema layer reports \code{skipped},
with a note, never \code{pass}.
}
}

